es-should-be-explicit\n- connections/git-scope-errors-inflate-the-work-estimate\n- connections/ground-truth-checks-must-match-the-lying-layer\n- connections/hindsight-leakage-and-pregame-freeze-capture\n- connections/hook-execution-environment-path-assumptions\n- connections/inbox-triage-and-morning-briefing-taxonomy\n- connections/install-time-capture-goes-stale-when-host-moves\n- connections/install-time-environment-capture-rots-silently\n- connections/instrumentation-that-normalizes-the-thing-under-test\n- connections/keep-data-retrievable-but-out-of-standing-context\n- connections/keep-it-retrievable-not-resident\n- connections/mandatory-routing-language-outranks-runtime-capability\n- connections/memory-compiler-tier-independence-under-failure\n- connections/mrsi-noise-vs-narrative-reliance\n- connections/multi-stage-review-catches-agent-blind-spots\n- connections/name-and-flag-are-not-the-artifact\n- connections/narrowest-sufficient-escalation-over-blanket-bypass\n- connections/newer-is-not-more-complete\n- connections/obsidian-vault-as-knowledge-hub\n- connections/preconditions-that-check-the-wrong-thing\n- connections/preserve-the-prior-version-before-restyling\n- connections/refuse-at-the-earliest-unmet-precondition\n- connections/refuse-upstream-or-verify-downstream\n- connections/report-the-gap-rather-than-the-fraction\n- connections/retrieval-score-vs-advisor-grounding\n- connections/reviewer-unavailability-as-a-forcing-function\n- connections/reviewers-cannot-audit-decision-order\n- connections/silent-omission-defeats-per-stage-success\n- connections/sports-tech-vendor-integration-friction\n- connections/status-field-scope-narrower-than-the-question\n- connections/step-down-to-ground-truth-when-the-surface-lies\n- connections/substitute-a-primitive-when-the-layer-is-unreliable\n- connections/the-default-is-the-real-decision\n- connections/the-monitor-is-the-defect\n- connections/the-preservation-step-must-itself-be-verified\n- connections/the-reference-window-decides-the-verdict\n- connections/unactioned-urgent-items-age-across-digests\n- connections/vald-forcedecks-cmj-data-across-projects\n- connections/vault-constraints-shape-tooling-design\n- connections/vendor-action-bypasses-os-setting\n- connections/verify-before-trusting-tool-output\n- connections/web-scraping-skill-and-readme-doc-trick\n- connections/windows-bash-tool-no-output-powershell-fallback\n- connections/windows-defaults-outlive-the-windows-machine\n- connections/windows-process-env-fixed-at-launch\n\n## Candidate 1\n(they share 6 neighbours; lexical overlap 0.04; written 138 days apart)\n\n### A: connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Connection: Aggregate Success Hides a Shrunken Work Set\"\nconnects:\n  - \"concepts/on-demand-backup-skill-and-ssd-completeness\"\n  - \"concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag\"\n  - \"concepts/vendored-skill-hand-install-pattern\"\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-04.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-08-31.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-04\nupdated: 2026-09-07\n---\n\n# Connection: Aggregate Success Hides a Shrunken Work Set\n\n## The Connection\n\nThree findings, two of them from 2026-09-04, share a defect that no per-item check can catch. A backup ran with CRASHBOX unmounted and reported zero errors while completing only 9 of its 31 jobs — every job it attempted succeeded, and the missing 22 were never attempted, so nothing failed. A backup manifest on 2026-08-31 omitted three project folders entirely; again every listed folder backed up cleanly. And five hand-installed skills on 2026-09-04 all work perfectly while being absent from `~/.agents/.skill-lock.json`, so `skills update` will never see them. In each case the item that matters is missing from the set the checker iterates over, which means the checker is structurally incapable of noticing it.\n\n## Key Insight\n\nSuccess reporting is almost always computed over the work set, not over the requirement. That makes the aggregate verdict a statement about *execution* when the operator reads it as a statement about *coverage* — and the two diverge precisely when something has silently dropped out of scope. The consequence is that error counts are the wrong instrument for this class of failure and cardinality is the right one: 9 versus 31 jobs is legible where \"0 errors\" is not, because a count can be compared against an expectation while a null error set cannot.\n\nThis gives a concrete design rule, and 2026-09-04 shows it being adopted: the new `/backup` skill reports skipped targets *explicitly* rather than folding them into an aggregate, converting an unobservable absence into a visible line item. The generalization is that any process operating over an enumerated set should publish the cardinality of that set alongside its verdict, and should name what it declined or could not reach. Where the set is derived from a hand-maintained list — a backup manifest, a lockfile — the deeper fix is to derive it from a scan instead, so membership is a consequence of reality rather than of someone having remembered.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- 2026-09-04 (16:17): \"Backup job count is a useful completeness signal: 9 jobs OK without the SSD vs 31 jobs OK with it. A '0 errors' run can still be silently incomplete when a target is unmounted, so the count matters more than the error count.\" The uncovered data was exactly the class with no second copy — uncommitted, non-repo, and gitignored files.\n- 2026-08-31: three project folders \"missing from the hand-maintained backup manifest… a backup whose scope is wrong still reports per-folder success for the folders it knows about,\" with the recorded follow-up asking whether the manifest should be auto-derived from `~/.claude/projects` rather than curated by hand — [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]].\n- 2026-09-04 (13:18): \"Hand-installed skills are absent from `~/.agents/.skill-lock.json`, so `skills update` won't see them.\" All five skills were tested working; their invisibility is purely a membership fact.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/on-demand-backup-skill-and-ssd-completeness]] — The job-count signal and the skill's explicit skipped-target reporting.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]] — Manifest omission as a coverage failure distinct from any execution failure.\n- [[concepts/vendored-skill-hand-install-pattern]] — Working artifacts outside the manifest that governs their maintenance.\n- [[connections/silent-omission-defeats-per-stage-success]] — The same structure stated at the pipeline-stage level.\n- [[connections/status-field-scope-narrower-than-the-question]] — The adjacent failure where the field is accurate but answers a narrower predicate than the one asked.\n- [[connections/failure-notification-shared-dependency]] — A related reporting-layer blind spot, where the alarm itself is inside the failure's scope.\n\n```\n\n### B: connections/verify-before-trusting-tool-output\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Connection: Verify Directly, Don't Trust the Intermediate Layer\"\nconnects:\n  - \"concepts/sportscience-research-repo-batch-import\"\n  - \"concepts/the-monitor-newsletter\"\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-04-22.md\"\ncreated: 2026-04-22\nupdated: 2026-04-22\n---\n\n# Connection: Verify Directly, Don't Trust the Intermediate Layer\n\n## The Connection\n\nBoth substantive sessions on 2026-04-22 ran into a version of the same problem: a tool or agent produced output that *looked* fine - a citation with a plausible author list, a photo wired into HTML with a correct relative path - but was actually wrong or unverifiable through the usual checking method. In [[concepts/sportscience-research-repo-batch-import]], a background repair agent's citation fixes for IDs 286, 287, and 358 contained hallucinated author lists despite correctly identifying the papers' topics. In [[concepts/the-monitor-newsletter]], Blake Wright's photo failed to appear in the localhost preview server even though the HTML and file path were both correct.\n\n## Key Insight\n\nIn both cases, the fix was not to redo the original work but to change the *verification method* to something closer to the ground truth. The hallucinated citations were caught by extracting the actual PDF text with PyPDF2 and reading it directly, rather than trusting the agent's summarized output. The \"broken\" photo was confirmed to be fine by opening the HTML file directly in Chrome via `file://`, rather than trusting the brainstorm preview server's rendering. The shared lesson: when an agent, tool, or service produces output that looks plausible but can't be easily double-checked through the same channel, step down to a more primitive, lower-level check (raw file text, direct file rendering) before accepting the result as correct.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- 2026-04-22 (10:57): \"Background agent produced `docs/batch_r7_fixes.json` but hallucinated wrong author lists for IDs 286, 287, and 358... Used PyPDF2 text extraction to verify actual authors and corrected all three citations before committing.\"\n- 2026-04-22 (17:21): \"Photo failed to render in localhost preview — identified as a server limitation (brainstorm preview server doesn't serve image files), not an HTML error... always verify photo rendering by opening the HTML directly in Chrome via file:// URL.\"\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/sportscience-research-repo-batch-import]]\n- [[concepts/the-monitor-newsletter]]\n\n```\n## Candidate 2\n(they share 5 neighbours; lexical overlap 0.02; written 144 days apart)\n\n### A: concepts/cross-provider-review-gate\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Cross-Provider Review Gate: a Different Reviewer Catches a Different Error Class\"\naliases: [Codex usage limit mid-gate, --host codex provider switch, resume cannot cross providers, round 10 plan review, re-run the gate after amending, stale approval, second reviewer different provider]\ntags: [code-review, claude-code, codex, planning, verification, workflow, process]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-12.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-12\nupdated: 2026-09-13\n---\n\n# Cross-Provider Review Gate: a Different Reviewer Catches a Different Error Class\n\nOn 2026-09-12 a plan-review gate running under Codex hit a usage limit mid-review. The reviewer was switched to Claude via `--host codex`, which starts a fresh review session because a resume cannot cross providers. The substitute reviewer immediately found the plan's largest error — that 14 of 19 outward symlinks were third-party installs Task 4 would have wrongly copied — which the original reviewer had not surfaced across prior rounds. The forced substitution turned out to be the most productive event in the review.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The provider switch was a fallback that outperformed the original.** It was adopted because Codex was rate-limited until 4:46 PM, not because more coverage was wanted; the scope error it caught was the plan's biggest defect.\n- **A resume cannot cross providers.** Switching hosts discards the accumulated review session and starts clean — which is part of why the new reviewer saw the inventory with fresh eyes rather than inheriting the prior framing.\n- **Re-run the gate after amending a plan; prior approval is stale.** Codex found 5 new bugs in the amendment itself, meaning the edits introduced defects at a rate comparable to the original draft.\n- **The review reached round 10 without approval,** ending with one serious finding (a safety hook that silently breaks the daily backup), and two minor ones (`.gitignore` vs `.git/info/exclude` wording, and a Pi test user likely inheriting the `staff` group).\n- **The stated generalization:** a second reviewer with a different provider catches different classes of error, so provider diversity is a coverage mechanism rather than merely a redundancy one.\n\n## Details\n\nThe finding refines a pattern already established in this knowledge base. [[concepts/subagent-driven-development]] documented that splitting review into a spec-compliance pass and a code-quality pass catches bugs that either pass alone would miss, and [[connections/multi-stage-review-catches-agent-blind-spots]] generalized that to differently-focused reviewers. What 2026-09-12 adds is that the *provider* is itself a lens: two reviewers given the same instructions and the same artifact, differing only in the model behind them, surfaced non-overlapping defects. The Codex rounds had converged on implementation-level findings; the Claude round asked a question no prior round had — who owns each of these 19 files — and the answer invalidated an entire task. Diversity of lens can therefore come from diversity of substrate, not only from diversity of instruction.\n\nThe staleness rule is the more immediately actionable half. An approved plan that is then amended is not an approved plan, and the temptation to treat the amendment as a small delta below the review threshold is exactly what the five new bugs refute. Amendments are written under time pressure, against a mental model of the plan that the reviewer no longer shares, and they modify precisely the regions that review already identified as fragile. Re-running the gate costs another round; not re-running it means the final artifact's most recently edited sections are its least reviewed. This is the same reasoning that makes a verification step worthless if it runs before the last mutation, and it complements the caution in [[connections/reviewers-cannot-audit-decision-order]] about what layered review structurally *cannot* catch — here the concern is the opposite, that review can catch it but was simply not re-run.\n\nIt is worth noting what the ten rounds cost and what they bought. The serious round-10 finding concerns a safety hook that would silently break the daily backup: a refused commit is ignored but still logged as \"committed,\" and the blocked link stays staged, so every subsequent backup fails without announcement. That is a defect whose entire character is silence — a monitor reporting success over a broken operation, the shape catalogued in [[connections/the-monitor-is-the-defect]] and [[concepts/background-monitor-signal-reliability]]. Finding it at round 10 rather than in production is the review gate's justification; finding it only after a provider switch and an amendment cycle is the argument for both practices.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/harness-agnostic-aios-plan]] — The plan under review, and the Task 4 scope error the substitute reviewer caught.\n- [[concepts/subagent-driven-development]] — The two-stage review methodology this extends, with provider as an additional axis of lens diversity.\n- [[connections/multi-stage-review-catches-agent-blind-spots]] — The general principle that a differently-focused second pass finds what the first structurally could not.\n- [[connections/reviewer-unavailability-as-a-forcing-function]] — The prior case where a blocked reviewer forced a substitution; here the substitution improved the outcome rather than merely preserving it.\n- [[connections/the-monitor-is-the-defect]] — The class the serious round-10 finding belongs to: a status report that says \"committed\" over a refused commit.\n- [[concepts/codex-adversarial-verification]] — Prior adversarial-review practice with Codex, including rate-limit routing.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-12.md]] — Memory maintenance session (17:31): Codex usage limit mid-gate (reset 4:46 PM), reviewer switched to Claude via `--host codex` starting a fresh session because resume cannot cross providers, the Claude reviewer's discovery that 14 of 19 outward symlinks are third-party, the three round-10 findings, the lesson that a plan must be re-reviewed after amendment (5 new bugs found in the amendment), and the conclusion that a second reviewer with a different provider catches different classes of error.\n\n```\n\n### B: connections/verify-before-trusting-tool-output\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Connection: Verify Directly, Don't Trust the Intermediate Layer\"\nconnects:\n  - \"concepts/sportscience-research-repo-batch-import\"\n  - \"concepts/the-monitor-newsletter\"\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-04-22.md\"\ncreated: 2026-04-22\nupdated: 2026-04-22\n---\n\n# Connection: Verify Directly, Don't Trust the Intermediate Layer\n\n## The Connection\n\nBoth substantive sessions on 2026-04-22 ran into a version of the same problem: a tool or agent produced output that *looked* fine - a citation with a plausible author list, a photo wired into HTML with a correct relative path - but was actually wrong or unverifiable through the usual checking method. In [[concepts/sportscience-research-repo-batch-import]], a background repair agent's citation fixes for IDs 286, 287, and 358 contained hallucinated author lists despite correctly identifying the papers' topics. In [[concepts/the-monitor-newsletter]], Blake Wright's photo failed to appear in the localhost preview server even though the HTML and file path were both correct.\n\n## Key Insight\n\nIn both cases, the fix was not to redo the original work but to change the *verification method* to something closer to the ground truth. The hallucinated citations were caught by extracting the actual PDF text with PyPDF2 and reading it directly, rather than trusting the agent's summarized output. The \"broken\" photo was confirmed to be fine by opening the HTML file directly in Chrome via `file://`, rather than trusting the brainstorm preview server's rendering. The shared lesson: when an agent, tool, or service produces output that looks plausible but can't be easily double-checked through the same channel, step down to a more primitive, lower-level check (raw file text, direct file rendering) before accepting the result as correct.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- 2026-04-22 (10:57): \"Background agent produced `docs/batch_r7_fixes.json` but hallucinated wrong author lists for IDs 286, 287, and 358... Used PyPDF2 text extraction to verify actual authors and corrected all three citations before committing.\"\n- 2026-04-22 (17:21): \"Photo failed to render in localhost preview — identified as a server limitation (brainstorm preview server doesn't serve image files), not an HTML error... always verify photo rendering by opening the HTML directly in Chrome via file:// URL.\"\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/sportscience-research-repo-batch-import]]\n- [[concepts/the-monitor-newsletter]]\n\n```\n## Candidate 3\n(they share 7 neighbours; lexical overlap 0.098; written 38 days apart)\n\n### A: concepts/automation-stack-doctor\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Automation Stack Doctor and Self-Maintaining Scheduled-Task Cycle\"\naliases: [stack doctor, KeystoneOS-PostRestartCheck, post-restart health check, self-deleting scheduled task, nightly self-maintaining cycle, daily-backup.ps1, backup execution limit, cloud-check UTC bug, stack doctor ALL CLEAR]\ntags: [claude-code, automation, windows, task-scheduler, memory, infrastructure, maintenance, scheduling, powershell]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-07-27.md\"\ncreated: 2026-07-27\nupdated: 2026-08-02\n---\n\n# Automation Stack Doctor and Self-Maintaining Scheduled-Task Cycle\n\nThe automation stack doctor is a health-check routine that verifies the full local Claude Code automation and memory stack — scheduled tasks, Python venvs, memsearch, Docker/Milvus, the error log, backups, and the Google Drive API heartbeat — and reports a consolidated status. On 2026-07-27, following a Windows reboot, a post-restart stack doctor run returned ALL CLEAR, and the session restructured the stack into a self-maintaining nightly cycle: heavy reindexing was moved into the 6:15 AM backup task while the session-end hook stays a light 45-second incremental pass.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Stack doctor scope:** a single run validates all 4 scheduled tasks, Python venvs, memsearch, Docker/Milvus survival across restart, error-log cleanliness, backup freshness, and a Google Drive API heartbeat. The 2026-07-27 post-restart run returned ALL CLEAR (backups fresh at 3h, Milvus survived reboot).\n- **Self-deleting post-restart check:** a one-shot scheduled task (`KeystoneOS-PostRestartCheck`, triggered at logon + 5 min) auto-runs stack doctor after a reboot, escalates any failures to `.errors.log`, and deletes itself. This is the workaround for the fact that a session cannot literally wait through a machine restart — failures surface at the next session start via the error log.\n- **Nightly reindex folded into the 6:15 AM backup task** (`daily-backup.ps1`): uncapped, uses the full `memsearch.exe` path (per the Task Scheduler PATH lesson), escalates per-path failures, and resets the hook timeout-streak counter on success. Rationale: a first-time SecondBrain book-library index is an hours-scale job that a 45-second session-end hook can never absorb.\n- **Backup task execution limit raised 30 min → 2 h** so a heavy reindex night is not killed mid-pass. The session-end hook stays light (45 s incremental) and its alarm threshold was raised 3 → 6 so it only alarms if the nightly safety net itself falls behind — a real signal, not noise.\n- **Cloud-check timezone bug fixed:** a \"-4h old\" false backup-freshness reading came from timestamps not normalized to UTC after `ConvertFrom-Json`; the check now normalizes to UTC regardless of the return type.\n\n## Details\n\nThe session's central move is an architectural relocation of expensive work off the interactive/hook path and onto a scheduled job. The recurring memsearch indexing alarm had been misdiagnosed the prior day as an \"occasional\" hiccup; on 2026-07-27 it was recognized as structural — a first-time embedding job measured in hours (one indexer had burned 81 CPU-minutes and was still running). Because a 45-second session-end hook can never complete an hours-scale index, the correct home for that work is the nightly 6:15 AM `daily-backup.ps1` task, which runs uncapped and escalates per-path failures. The session-end hook is left as a light incremental pass, and its alarm threshold is raised from 3 to 6 consecutive misses so it only fires when the nightly safety net itself falls behind. With the library index converging over a night or two while search stays live, the alarm becomes a meaningful signal rather than chronic noise.\n\nTwo supporting reliability fixes accompanied the restructure. The backup task's execution time limit was raised from 30 minutes to 2 hours so a heavy reindex night is not terminated mid-pass — a scheduled job that runs an unbounded index needs an execution ceiling wide enough to absorb the worst case. The cloud-check UTC normalization fix addressed a false \"-4h old\" reading: after `ConvertFrom-Json`, timestamps were being compared without normalizing to UTC, so a local-vs-UTC offset was misread as staleness; normalizing regardless of the parsed return type eliminated the false alarm. The `KeystoneOS-PostRestartCheck` self-deleting task closes the post-reboot verification gap — because no session can survive a restart, a one-shot logon-triggered task that runs stack doctor and escalates to `.errors.log` is the mechanism by which post-restart health reaches the user at the next session start. All changes were committed and pushed to the dotclaude and skills repos with no pending user action; the first fully self-maintaining cycle (backup → snapshot → reindex → alarm reset) was scheduled to run the following morning at 6:15 AM.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/memsearch-tool-maintenance]] — The memsearch tool whose hours-scale first-index drove this restructure; the nightly reindex-in-backup-task architecture and the raised 3→6 alarm threshold are documented there as well.\n- [[concepts/windows-task-scheduler-path-config]] — The full-`memsearch.exe`-path requirement applied to the nightly reindex task is a direct application of this Task Scheduler minimal-PATH lesson.\n- [[concepts/in-session-background-process-limits]] — The architectural rationale for moving hours-scale reindexing out of the session entirely: long-running background commands do not survive a session, so scheduled jobs are their correct home.\n- [[concepts/system-health-check-remediation]] — An earlier single-pass health-check-and-remediation session on the same stack; the stack doctor generalizes that periodic triage discipline into an automated, scheduled routine.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-07-27.md]] — Post-restart stack doctor session (16:40): ALL CLEAR result, `KeystoneOS-PostRestartCheck` self-deleting logon task registered, nightly reindex moved into 6:15 AM `daily-backup.ps1`, session-end hook left at 45 s with alarm threshold raised 3→6, backup execution limit raised 30 min→2 h, cloud-check UTC normalization bug fixed, PR #16 merged (`59b6f2c`).\n\n```\n\n### B: concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Closing a 49-Folder Backup Manifest Coverage Gap\"\naliases: [49 unmanifested DEV folders, backup-manifest.json 78 to 125, job count 28 to 76, manifest coverage gap, AI_Analyst token-dashboard removed, DexaProject Google Drive warning, migration left folders unmanifested]\ntags: [backup, automation, macos, keystoneos, verification, maintenance, git]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-09.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-09\nupdated: 2026-09-09\n---\n\n# Closing a 49-Folder Backup Manifest Coverage Gap\n\nOn 2026-09-09 a KeystoneOS daily backup run returned `status: ERROR` with two problems: the `~/.claude` repo was one commit behind GitHub (blocking its push) and 49 folders under `~/DEV` were absent from `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json`. Rather than triage the 49, the user confirmed that all of them mattered — the gap was a residue of an earlier migration — and every one was added. The manifest grew from 78 to 125 entries and the backup's job count went from 28 to 76.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The gap was invisible.** Folders absent from the manifest are never backed up, and their absence produces no error that reads as urgent — only an advisory `manifest-unmanaged` class. The 49-folder hole had existed since a prior migration.\n- **All 49 were added, not triaged:** 47 as git repos (working-tree capture to the CRASHBOX SSD) and 2 as plain folders (`_ARCHIVE`, `docs` — SSD copy only, no repo semantics).\n- **Two stale entries were removed** (`AI_Analyst`, `token-dashboard`) after confirming both had already moved into `~/DEV/_ARCHIVE/`, leaving 125 entries. Manifest hygiene runs in both directions: adding new folders and retiring moved ones.\n- **`DexaProject`'s warning was kept deliberately** — it lives on Google Drive intentionally, so its manifest complaint is an accepted permanent condition rather than a defect to silence. A separate later finding in the same day established that the *GitHub* copy of `DexaProject` still holds a patient CSV and must be deleted.\n- **The `~/.claude` behind-by-one state was resolved with `git pull --rebase --autostash`,** preserving two uncommitted edits (`settings.json`, `hooks/herdr-agent-state.sh`). Backups never auto-merge by design.\n- **Commits were made but not pushed** — `~/.claude` at `dc54c97` and `~/.claude/global-memory` at `38e462b` — with the push deliberately left to the next scheduled backup run.\n\n## Details\n\nThis is the largest instance yet of the manifest-drift failure class first catalogued in [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]] (three folders) and re-observed at [[concepts/backup-error-log-recurring-failures-2026-08-16]] (five folders). The escalation from three to five to forty-nine is informative: the earlier gaps were ordinary drift from folders created faster than manifest entries, whereas this one had a single structural cause — a migration that moved or created many directories at once while the manifest, being hand-maintained, stayed where it was. Bulk moves are precisely the event a curated list cannot survive, which strengthens the standing proposal in [[concepts/dev-folder-manifest-default-deny]] to derive the manifest from a directory scan rather than maintaining it by hand. The default-deny guard did its job in that the folders were reported rather than silently excluded, but reporting alone is only sufficient when the report is acted on, and forty-nine entries accumulating across weeks shows the report being observed and deferred.\n\nThe removal of `AI_Analyst` and `token-dashboard` is the neglected half of manifest maintenance. A manifest entry pointing at a folder that has moved into `_ARCHIVE` will either fail loudly (noise) or resolve to a stale path (worse), and neither outcome is caught by any check that only asks \"is every folder covered?\" The `DexaProject` decision demonstrates the third state a manifest entry can occupy: an accepted, documented exception whose warning is expected output rather than a pending task. Recording the reason — it lives on Google Drive intentionally — is what prevents a future maintenance pass from either \"fixing\" it or being confused by it, the same discipline applied to the two large graph files kept deliberately in the 2026-09-01 classification pass. Notably, the same day's repo-visibility audit found that this deliberate local exception coexists with a genuine problem at a different layer: `erash11/DexaProject` is still a GitHub repository containing a patient CSV, awaiting deletion. A documented exception on one axis does not clear the artifact on every axis.\n\nThe `git pull --rebase --autostash` choice is worth isolating as policy. The backup job pushes several repos, and a repo that is behind its remote cannot push; the tempting resolutions are a force-push (destroys remote history) or a merge (creates a commit the operator did not author). Rebasing with autostash preserves both the remote's history and the two uncommitted working-tree edits, and the stated principle — backups never auto-merge — keeps the backup system from making content decisions on the operator's behalf. This is the same restraint shown when a two-writer SecondBrain conflict was resolved by staging both sides rather than adjudicating it, per [[concepts/stack-doctor-sweep-2026-08-11]]. The decision to commit `~/.claude` and `global-memory` without pushing follows the same logic in the other direction: the push is the backup's job, so leaving it to the next scheduled run keeps one owner for that action rather than racing it.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/dev-folder-manifest-default-deny]] — The fail-closed guard that surfaced the 49 folders; this run is the strongest argument yet for its auto-derivation follow-up.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]] — The original three-folder instance of the same failure class.\n- [[concepts/backup-error-log-recurring-failures-2026-08-16]] — The five-folder `manifest-unmanaged` instance between that one and this.\n- [[concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count]] — The completeness metric this session reset from 28 to 76.\n- [[concepts/on-demand-backup-skill-and-ssd-completeness]] — The job-count completeness tell, which this session extends and complicates by moving the baseline.\n- [[concepts/crashbox-ssd-backup-baseline]] — The SSD leg that now receives working-tree captures for 47 additional repos.\n- [[concepts/public-repo-privacy-audit-read-inside]] — The same day's finding that `DexaProject` is also a live public GitHub repo holding a patient CSV.\n- [[concepts/stack-doctor-sweep-2026-08-11]] — The precedent for resolving repo divergence without adjudicating content on the operator's behalf.\n- [[connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set]] — The general shape: a clean run over a work set that was never complete.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-09.md]] — Backup repair session (19:17): initial run `status: ERROR` with `~/.claude` one commit behind and 49 unmanifested `~/DEV` folders; user confirmed all 49 mattered (\"we did a migration earlier\"); 47 added as git repos and 2 as plain folders (`_ARCHIVE`, `docs`); job count 28 → 76 and manifest 78 → 125 entries; `AI_Analyst` and `token-dashboard` removed after confirming both had moved to `~/DEV/_ARCHIVE/`; `DexaProject` warning kept as an intentional Google Drive exception; `~/.claude` fixed with `git pull --rebase --autostash` preserving two uncommitted edits; commits `dc54c97` and `38e462b` left unpushed for the next backup run.\n\n```\n## Candidate 4\n(they share 6 neighbours; lexical overlap 0.099; written 42 days apart)\n\n### A: concepts/in-session-background-process-limits\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"In-Session Background Processes Don't Survive; Hours-Scale Work Belongs in Scheduled Jobs\"\naliases: [background process killed in session, hours-scale work scheduled job, orphaned indexer processes, session background command limits, long-running work architecture]\ntags: [claude-code, automation, architecture, windows, task-scheduler, memory, infrastructure, gotcha]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-07-27.md\"\ncreated: 2026-07-27\nupdated: 2026-07-27\n---\n\n# In-Session Background Processes Don't Survive; Hours-Scale Work Belongs in Scheduled Jobs\n\nLong-running background processes started from within a Claude Code session do not reliably survive the session — when the session ends they are terminated (status `killed`), and any child work they spawned can be left orphaned. On 2026-07-27, this was demonstrated when foreground memsearch reindex tasks were killed twice, leaving orphaned indexer processes that had to be cleaned up. The architectural conclusion: work measured in hours (like a first-time embedding index) belongs in a scheduled OS-level job, not an in-session background command.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Session-scoped processes are killed with the session:** a background command launched inside a session shares the session's lifetime; when the session terminates, the process is killed (observed status `killed`), regardless of whether its work completed.\n- **Killed background jobs can orphan children:** the 2026-07-27 reindex tasks were killed twice, each time leaving orphaned indexer processes still holding CPU that had to be manually cleaned up — a killed parent does not guarantee a clean child teardown.\n- **Hours-scale work must be relocated to a scheduled job:** a first-time SecondBrain book-library index is an hours-scale job (one indexer burned 81 CPU-minutes and was still going); the correct home is a Windows scheduled task (the 6:15 AM `daily-backup.ps1`), which runs independently of any session.\n- **Sessions cannot wait through discontinuities:** a session cannot wait through a machine restart, nor block for hours on an index; the general workaround is to hand the work to a scheduled task and surface results/failures asynchronously (e.g., via `.errors.log` escalation read at the next session start).\n\n## Details\n\nThe failure is structural, not incidental. A Claude Code session's process tree is tied to the session's lifetime, so any background command it launches is a candidate for termination when the session ends or is interrupted. This is acceptable for short tasks that finish within a turn, but it is fundamentally mismatched to work whose runtime exceeds a session — an hours-scale first-time embedding index cannot complete before the session that started it is likely gone. Worse, a killed background parent does not guarantee its own descendants are reaped: on 2026-07-27 the killed reindex tasks left orphaned indexer processes consuming CPU, requiring an explicit cleanup pass. The lesson is that the session is the wrong execution context for durable, long-running work.\n\nThe remedy is to move such work to an OS-level scheduler that owns its own lifetime and execution ceiling — in this case the nightly 6:15 AM backup task, which runs uncapped by the session, with an execution limit widened (30 min → 2 h) to absorb a heavy reindex night. This is the same reasoning that motivates the self-deleting post-restart health-check task: since a session cannot literally wait through a reboot, a logon-triggered scheduled task performs the post-restart verification and escalates failures asynchronously. In both cases the pattern is identical — take work that outlives or straddles a session boundary, hand it to a scheduled task, and reconcile the outcome later through a durable artifact (a log, an alarm counter, an escalation file) rather than expecting the session to hold state across the gap.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/automation-stack-doctor]] — The session where hours-scale reindexing was relocated from in-session background commands into the nightly 6:15 AM backup task; this concept is the architectural rationale for that move.\n- [[concepts/memsearch-tool-maintenance]] — The specific hours-scale index (memsearch first-time embedding) that could not survive in a session and drove the scheduled-job architecture.\n- [[connections/background-task-exit-code-false-failure]] — A companion lesson on background-task reliability: exit codes and status notifications from detached tasks are intermediate signals to be reconciled against on-disk artifacts, not trusted directly.\n- [[concepts/windows-task-scheduler-path-config]] — The scheduled-job home for relocated work carries its own environment constraints (full executable paths under a minimal PATH).\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-07-27.md]] — Post-restart stack doctor session (16:40): foreground reindex tasks killed twice leaving orphaned indexer processes; hours-scale first-index (81 CPU-minutes) recognized as needing a scheduled job, not an in-session background command; self-deleting post-restart task as the analogous workaround for waiting through a reboot.\n\n```\n\n### B: concepts/on-demand-backup-skill-and-ssd-completeness\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"The /backup Skill: Job Count as the Completeness Signal\"\naliases: [/backup skill, CRASHBOX not mounted, 9 jobs vs 31 jobs, CAPTURE and STATE-SSD job classes, diskutil list external empty, charge-only USB-C cable, run-result file not exit code, errors.log archived-2026-09-04, skill loads immediately no restart]\ntags: [backup, automation, macos, skills, hardware, verification, maintenance]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-04.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-09-07.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-04\nupdated: 2026-09-07\n---\n\n# The /backup Skill: Job Count as the Completeness Signal\n\nOn 2026-09-04 at 16:17, a partially-failed scheduled backup was re-run manually and the experience was turned into a reusable `/backup` skill. The session's central finding is a measurement lesson: a backup run reporting zero errors can still be silently incomplete when a target volume is unmounted, so the number of jobs completed — 9 without the CRASHBOX SSD attached versus 31 with it — is a better health signal than the error count. A secondary thread diagnosed the SSD's non-appearance as a charge-only USB-C cable rather than any software fault. On 2026-09-07 the tell was written into the skill file itself, with the mechanism identified and verified against real logs.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The scheduled job was fine; the network was not.** `com.keystone.dailybackup` fired on time at 09:05. Two repo pushes (`global-memory`, `skills`) failed from a transient network/DNS blip, and a manual re-run cleared them — the `.errors.log` entry described a symptom, not a broken scheduler.\n- **Zero errors ≠ complete.** With CRASHBOX unmounted, committed work still reached GitHub and Google Drive, but uncommitted, non-repo, and gitignored files were saved *nowhere*. The run reported no errors because every job it knew to attempt succeeded.\n- **Job count is the completeness metric:** 9 jobs OK without the SSD, 31 jobs OK with it. The gap is the entire local-only-file leg of the backup.\n- **The mechanism behind the tell (recorded 2026-09-07):** the `CAPTURE` and `STATE-SSD` job classes only exist when the drive is mounted, so their absence is what shrinks the count. Verified against two real runs — a 13:25 run without the SSD produced 4 repo pushes plus 6 Drive snaps (10 jobs), and a 13:46 run with it produced the same 10 plus 20 `CAPTURE` and 9 `STATE-SSD` jobs. This tell is now documented in `~/.claude/skills/backup/SKILL.md` rather than living only in the operator's head.\n- **The skill checks the run-result file, not exit codes,** reports skipped targets explicitly, and clears the stale `.errors.log` entry so session-start stops nagging about a resolved condition.\n- **A USB-C drive that powers on but never mounts is almost always a charge-only cable.** `diskutil list external` printing nothing at all — no external disk visible whatsoever — is the fast discriminator between a cable/port problem and a filesystem problem. Swapping the cable fixed it.\n- **A newly written skill file loads immediately** — no session restart is needed before invoking it.\n- Naming followed the nearest sibling: lowercase-kebab `backup` to match `claude-stack-doctor`, since no `_ALLCAPS` private skills exist in this workspace.\n\n## Details\n\nThe unmounted-target failure is the most transferable finding, and it is a specific instance of a status field answering a narrower question than the operator's. \"Did the backup succeed?\" was answered as \"did every attempted job succeed?\", which is true and useless when the set of attempted jobs is silently smaller than the set of required jobs. The nine-versus-thirty-one gap is not a partial degradation of coverage — it is precisely the class of data that has no other copy anywhere. Committed repository content is redundantly protected by GitHub and Google Drive, so the SSD leg exists specifically to capture uncommitted work, non-repo files, and gitignored artifacts. Losing exactly that leg while reporting a clean run is the worst possible arrangement of signal and reality, and it is why the new skill reports skipped targets explicitly rather than folding them into an aggregate verdict. This is the same defect shape as the backup manifest omission documented in [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]]: in both cases coverage was wrong while execution was perfect, and in both cases the aggregate success indicator was structurally incapable of noticing.\n\nThe 2026-09-07 follow-up hardened the tell from a remembered number into a documented, mechanism-backed check. Knowing that \"31 means good, 9 means bad\" is fragile — the totals shift as repos and Drive targets are added — whereas knowing that the `CAPTURE` and `STATE-SSD` job classes are conditional on the mount makes the check robust to changes in the other job counts. Writing it into `SKILL.md` also moves the knowledge to where the next operator (or the next session) will encounter it, rather than requiring recall. The verification method matters too: rather than reasoning from the manifest about what *should* run, the claim was checked against two real log entries taken minutes apart under both conditions, which is a natural A/B that isolates exactly the SSD-dependent jobs.\n\nThe hardware diagnosis contributes a small, reusable triage rule. When an external drive shows power indicators but never mounts, the instinct is to suspect the filesystem, the mount daemon, or permissions. `diskutil list external` returning *nothing* rules all of those out at once: a drive with a corrupt filesystem still enumerates as a device, so total absence from the device list places the fault below the software layer entirely — at the cable, the port, or the hub. USB-C makes this especially common because charge-only cables are physically identical to data-capable ones. The prescribed order — swap to a known data cable first, then change port or connect directly rather than through a hub — moves from cheapest to most disruptive, and the cable swap resolved it here.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]] — The closest prior instance: coverage failure with clean execution, and a status field scoped more narrowly than the operational question.\n- [[concepts/crashbox-ssd-backup-baseline]] — The first full baseline of this same SSD leg; documents the rsync-incremental behavior that makes re-running after a missed run cheap.\n- [[concepts/git-sync-status-versus-uncommitted-work]] — Why the SSD leg is the only protection during a deferred-commit window, when `ahead=0` reads green over an uncommitted working tree.\n- [[concepts/stale-error-log-archival-discipline]] — The archive-then-clear discipline the skill automates for `.errors.log`.\n- [[concepts/automation-stack-doctor]] — The sibling health-check skill whose naming convention `/backup` followed, and which surfaces backup freshness as a standing check.\n- [[concepts/offline-capable-pipeline-legs]] — The per-leg network-dependency framing; the transient push failure and the unmounted SSD are failures in two different legs with different remedies.\n- [[connections/background-task-exit-code-false-failure]] — The general rule the skill implements: reconcile against a durable artifact rather than trusting an exit code.\n- [[connections/status-field-scope-narrower-than-the-question]] — The \"0 errors\" verdict answering a narrower predicate than \"is the backup complete?\".\n- [[connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set]] — The shared shape across backup jobs, manifest folders, and lockfile-invisible skills.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-04.md]] — Manual backup and `/backup` skill session (16:17): `com.keystone.dailybackup` confirmed to have fired on time at 09:05 with two transient push failures cleared by a re-run, CRASHBOX unattached leaving uncommitted/non-repo/gitignored files unsaved, 9-versus-31 job count as the completeness signal, `diskutil list external` empty diagnosing a charge-only cable, lowercase-kebab naming after `claude-stack-doctor`, skill reading the run-result file and reporting skipped targets, stale `.errors.log` cleared with archive at `.errors.log.archived-2026-09-04`, immediate skill load without restart, and the final receipt `run-20260904-134638.txt` (7,801 files, 0 errors, 31 jobs OK).\n- [[daily/2026-09-07.md]] — Config-hygiene session (18:05): the job-count tell written into `~/.claude/skills/backup/SKILL.md`, mechanism identified as the `CAPTURE` and `STATE-SSD` job classes existing only when the drive is mounted, verified against a 13:25 run without the SSD (4 repo pushes + 6 Drive snaps) versus a 13:46 run with it (the same 10 plus 20 `CAPTURE` + 9 `STATE-SSD`), a stale `SKILL.md.bak-2026-08-29` deleted from the stack-doctor skill, and a `/claude-stack-doctor` run confirming backup OK with 31 targets.\n\n```\n\n## Output format\n\nFor EVERY candidate, in order, emit exactly one verdict block:\n\n===VERDICT===\npair: <A path> | <B path>\nreal: yes\nreason: <one sentence>\n===END VERDICT===\n\nFor each verdict where real is yes, ALSO emit a connection article:\n\n===ARTICLE connections/<slug>.md===\n<full markdown: YAML frontmatter (title, tags, sources, created, updated) then\nbody with Key Points (3-5 bullets), Details (2+ paragraphs), and Related\nConcepts linking BOTH source articles plus at least one other>\n===END ARTICLE===\n\nSlugs are lowercase and hyphenated and should name the relationship, not the\ntwo topics. After all articles, emit ONE index block containing one row per\narticle created:\n\n===INDEX===\n| [[connections/<slug>]] | one-line summary | adjudicated | 2026-09-13 |\n===END INDEX===\n\nEmit ONLY these blocks - no preamble, no commentary.\n"}
